14
00:00:55,500 --> 00:00:57,220
Randolph County, West Virginia.

15
00:00:57,520 --> 00:01:04,880
It's part of the 920,000 acres of Monongahela National Forest.

16
00:01:05,320 --> 00:01:08,200
It's home to nearly 4,000 American black bears.

17
00:01:10,880 --> 00:01:17,080
And every year, experts from the West Virginia Division of Natural Resources Black Bear Management Program

18
00:01:17,081 --> 00:01:22,020
are out to trap them as part of an ongoing statewide population study.

19
00:01:22,021 --> 00:01:28,580
The beastly challenge of trapping an animal that can weigh up to 400 pounds

20
00:01:28,581 --> 00:01:32,720
is handled with snares, a type of modified lasso.

21
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:36,700
They must be powerful enough to capture and restrain the bears,

22
00:01:37,620 --> 00:01:40,900
yet incorporate a humane design that won't injure them.

23
00:01:42,160 --> 00:01:44,880
The first step in building the snare is to pick a tree.

24
00:01:45,740 --> 00:01:49,120
When we're looking for the snare spot, we're looking for just that perfect tree,

25
00:01:49,300 --> 00:01:51,620
not one that's too big that we can't get the cable around.

26
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:54,300
not one too small that he's going to break it off the next morning.

27
00:01:54,540 --> 00:01:57,900
We're looking for a nice medium-sized tree, like this maple right here.

28
00:02:00,640 --> 00:02:03,400
Chris Ryan will use the tree as an anchor for the snare.

29
00:02:04,040 --> 00:02:06,020
He chains a wire loop to the trunk.

30
00:02:06,780 --> 00:02:09,380
This is a spring we put on the snares just for safety,

31
00:02:09,620 --> 00:02:13,240
just like on a porch swing, so that it's not just hooked to the tree real solid,

32
00:02:13,720 --> 00:02:15,280
so that the actual snare will give.

33
00:02:15,940 --> 00:02:19,280
Then you come out to the end of it, and this is just what we call the angle iron.

34
00:02:19,281 --> 00:02:23,860
And what we'll do is we'll point the actual cable on the outside.

35
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:29,340
You can see when the bear would be caught, the cables point out, so it's not cutting into the paw.

36
00:02:31,820 --> 00:02:40,120
The flat, even ground at the tree's base is perfect for securing the trap's key mechanical component, the throw arm.

37
00:02:41,560 --> 00:02:45,520
Throw arms come in various styles, but their mechanics never change.

38
00:02:46,520 --> 00:02:51,440
To set the snare, a trapper folds over the throw arm, which compresses a spring.

39
00:02:52,440 --> 00:02:56,500
A latch called the dog holds the arm down, which sets the trigger.

40
00:02:58,840 --> 00:03:01,520
Finally, the trapper connects the snare to the throw arm.

41
00:03:03,580 --> 00:03:09,980
When the unsuspecting bear steps on the trigger, the arm will fly back, tightening the snare around the bear's leg.

42
00:03:15,520 --> 00:03:18,160
The crew camouflages the trap with twigs and leaves.

43
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:25,240
A lot of times, too, what we'll try to do is we'll just give them a fake paw print out here in the front.

44
00:03:25,740 --> 00:03:27,560
Because they like to step where other bears will step.

45
00:03:29,360 --> 00:03:32,440
And what snack is tasty enough to lure a bear into their trap?

46
00:03:33,940 --> 00:03:36,960
Day-old donuts and a couple of greasy bacon strips.

47
00:03:38,720 --> 00:03:40,380
This is our secret weapon.

48
00:03:41,020 --> 00:03:44,860
We'll go over to a tree and let it pour down the tree.

49
00:03:44,861 --> 00:03:47,560
and in the bear you can see where he bites and claws at it.

50
00:03:50,180 --> 00:03:52,400
This snare, along with nine other traps,

51
00:03:52,660 --> 00:03:55,080
comprise a trap line spanning several miles,

52
00:03:55,280 --> 00:03:57,220
which the team will work for ten days.

53
00:03:58,340 --> 00:04:01,600
But for now, it's time to retreat and wait.

54
00:04:08,460 --> 00:04:10,020
Six of the snares are empty.

55
00:04:11,720 --> 00:04:13,920
We're standing right now at trap site number one,

56
00:04:13,921 --> 00:04:17,020
where over the last two summers we've caught six here.

57
00:04:17,680 --> 00:04:19,820
But they don't come back every day.

58
00:04:21,580 --> 00:04:23,540
There's no sign a bear here either.

59
00:04:24,300 --> 00:04:25,760
So we're just going to reset this

60
00:04:25,761 --> 00:04:28,920
and give it one more night like this.

61
00:04:31,240 --> 00:04:33,820
But at trap site number 10...

62
00:04:33,821 --> 00:04:35,060
10-4, we got one.

63
00:04:35,420 --> 00:04:36,120
We'll be there in a minute.

64
00:04:38,860 --> 00:04:42,160
A 250-pound adult male awaits them.

65
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:48,600
The team must tranquilize him before they can begin collecting data.

66
00:04:56,140 --> 00:04:58,400
I don't think he liked that very well.

67
00:05:00,640 --> 00:05:03,240
After about 10 minutes, he's sleeping peacefully.

68
00:05:04,240 --> 00:05:07,080
And the team confirms his leg hasn't been injured in the snare.

69
00:05:10,080 --> 00:05:13,040
Then they take his measurements and attach his ear tags.

70
00:05:13,041 --> 00:05:15,540
which will identify him within the study.

71
00:05:16,380 --> 00:05:18,560
You can see this is the trap that we set yesterday.

72
00:05:19,340 --> 00:05:21,920
We've got this nice male in here that we've just worked up

73
00:05:21,921 --> 00:05:23,500
and everything worked out perfectly.

74
00:05:24,140 --> 00:05:25,840
You can see he's rotated all around the tree.

75
00:05:26,800 --> 00:05:29,100
You know, we said yesterday that we'd come back

76
00:05:29,101 --> 00:05:31,400
and there'd probably be some claw marks or bite marks on this tree

77
00:05:31,401 --> 00:05:32,280
and short shooting.

78
00:05:32,820 --> 00:05:34,060
There's a couple of marks on this tree.

79
00:05:34,660 --> 00:05:36,740
And you can see there's one final donut

80
00:05:36,741 --> 00:05:39,700
right outside of his circle that he couldn't reach.

81
00:05:39,701 --> 00:05:44,740
Every individual bear's data are valuable to the study.

82
00:05:48,480 --> 00:05:53,140
But that doesn't mean they appreciate being outwitted, especially this mother.

83
00:05:55,580 --> 00:05:56,280
Okay.

84
00:05:56,780 --> 00:05:58,640
She was captured with her cubs in tow.

85
00:05:59,800 --> 00:06:03,600
Now they cautiously look on, from up in the nearby trees.

86
00:06:03,601 --> 00:06:10,560
the management team will base its hunting season recommendations on the data collected from female

87
00:06:10,561 --> 00:06:16,040
captures she's okay now just let her be quiet now stand still and let her be quiet

