00:06:16,041 --> 00:06:25,080
we actually caught this bear last year at the same site we put a collar on her then

89
00:06:25,081 --> 00:06:31,800
and we followed her all fall and in the winter into the den so she did approximately two or

90
00:06:31,801 --> 00:06:36,520
three miles from here and had two cubs in March, and they'll stay with them for a year and a half.

91
00:06:37,880 --> 00:06:41,280
West Virginia law prohibits hunting a mother accompanied by cubs,

92
00:06:41,520 --> 00:06:45,160
but once they're on their own, she's fair game.

93
00:06:46,480 --> 00:06:51,820
We've got a tremendous bear population in these counties, and we can still allow people to hunt

94
00:06:51,821 --> 00:06:57,620
and to stabilize that population by setting those seasons to actually harvest some of the females,

95
00:06:57,621 --> 00:07:00,300
because those females are what control the population.

96
00:07:01,800 --> 00:07:08,800
Within an hour after her release, this mother bear rejoined her cubs.

97
00:07:10,800 --> 00:07:17,800
Over the 10-day study period, the team captured, tagged and released over 20 black bears.

98
00:07:19,800 --> 00:07:24,800
But historically, trapping has been geared more towards securing an animal's fur and meat

99
00:07:24,801 --> 00:07:28,800
than guaranteeing its species a future.

100
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:36,800
Thousands of years before the rifle, man hunted with traps.

101
00:07:36,801 --> 00:07:41,800
Many anthropologists believe one of the first traps was the pitfall.

102
00:07:41,801 --> 00:07:46,800
Primitive man used it to catch mammoth nearly 200,000 years ago.

103
00:07:46,801 --> 00:07:53,800
By simply digging a hole in the ground and covering it with sticks, dirt and leaves, they could catch a feast.

104
00:07:55,800 --> 00:08:00,800
As society advanced, innovators created increasingly sophisticated traps.

105
00:08:00,801 --> 00:08:06,800
traps to capture more elusive prey.

106
00:08:06,801 --> 00:08:13,800
Egyptian fowlers in the Nile Valley used clap nets to capture entire flocks of birds.

107
00:08:13,801 --> 00:08:21,800
They used two 12-foot long, 5-foot wide hexagonal nets, stretched over wooden frames, resting

108
00:08:21,801 --> 00:08:23,800
on an axis.

109
00:08:23,801 --> 00:08:29,800
A length of rope was connected to the sides of the trap with catgut strings.

110
00:08:29,801 --> 00:08:39,080
When birds landed on the net to feed, the fowlers yanked the rope, which caused the two sides of the trap to clap together, and captured the birds alive.

111
00:08:42,020 --> 00:08:49,880
The fowlers would trap huge flocks for food, decoration, religious sacrifice, and even as a means of pest control in their orchards.

112
00:08:49,881 --> 00:08:57,720
today the north american trap collectors association museum in galloway ohio

113
00:08:57,721 --> 00:09:07,060
preserves over 3 000 cunning and often deadly antique traps this snare is made of woven plant

114
00:09:07,061 --> 00:09:15,880
fibers comes from the philippines and is designed to catch chickens created in the 1800s and still

115
00:09:15,881 --> 00:09:22,284
in use today. His non-lethal trap catches birds around their necks.

116
00:09:22,285 --> 00:09:24,960
This is a live muskrat trap,

117
00:09:24,961 --> 00:09:27,930
and to set this, you would just raise the lid.

118
00:09:27,931 --> 00:09:30,900
The muskrat would float up to the board. You'd

119
00:09:30,901 --> 00:09:34,587
have bait in here. They would hit the trigger, and down it would go.

120
00:09:34,588 --> 00:09:35,780
They couldn't get out.

121
00:09:36,720 --> 00:09:40,720
The buoyant design keeps the muskrat from drowning.

122
00:09:40,721 --> 00:09:43,700
This is an early Eskimo lemming snare,

123
00:09:43,701 --> 00:09:48,960
and the actual snare device is made of whale baleen.

124
00:09:50,980 --> 00:09:54,760
Trapmakers have long been developing unique designs for all types of animals,

125
00:09:55,120 --> 00:09:58,640
including one species in 17th century America

126
00:09:58,641 --> 00:10:03,280
that played a key role in pushing the nation west, the beaver.

127
00:10:03,920 --> 00:10:08,420
The beaver was the most sought-after animal in the continent

128
00:10:08,421 --> 00:10:13,440
and the mountain man was gone after these guys full force.

129
00:10:13,700 --> 00:10:17,960
The quality of the pelt was such that once they got these tanned,

130
00:10:17,961 --> 00:10:23,080
they would pluck the outer hair and utilize the under fur

131
00:10:23,081 --> 00:10:26,880
and would make these top hats.

132
00:10:28,540 --> 00:10:32,020
And these were made for the well-to-do people in England

133
00:10:32,021 --> 00:10:36,500
and also in the eastern United States, New York, and so on.

134
00:10:38,540 --> 00:10:41,540
Rival fur trading companies fed the fever for Beaver.

135
00:10:41,541 --> 00:10:44,060
and by the early 1800s,

136
00:10:44,480 --> 00:10:46,320
their armies of mountain men were harvesting

137
00:10:46,321 --> 00:10:49,240
the 60-pound rodents with steel jaw traps.

138
00:10:51,480 --> 00:10:53,520
When the trapper compressed the springs,

139
00:10:54,020 --> 00:10:57,560
the jaws opened and were held with a latch called the dog.

140
00:10:59,540 --> 00:11:01,600
When a beaver stepped onto the pan,

141
00:11:02,160 --> 00:11:05,220
the dog was disengaged and the jaws snapped shut.

142
00:11:06,100 --> 00:11:08,180
A chain staked to the ground held the trap

143
00:11:08,181 --> 00:11:10,700
and the trapped animal in place.

144
00:11:11,540 --> 00:11:15,260
It was a powerful new tool in the hands of the mountain men.

145
00:11:16,300 --> 00:11:21,740
So powerful that by 1840, trappers had decimated the beaver population in North America.

146
00:11:23,260 --> 00:11:31,780
But their persistence and pioneering spirit opened the West to a flood of migrants, eager to stake their claims on the American frontier.

147
00:11:31,781 --> 00:11:42,300
In the 1860s, trapmaker Oneida Victor introduced a larger version of the steel jaw trap designed to capture grizzly bears.

148
00:11:44,120 --> 00:11:46,620
This is a number six Newhouse bear trap.

149
00:11:47,080 --> 00:11:49,740
The jaw spread on this trap is 16 and a half inches.

150
00:11:49,900 --> 00:11:53,280
The trap is 44 inches long and it weighs 50 pounds.

151
00:11:54,320 --> 00:11:58,060
The number six became rightfully known as the Great Bear Tamer.

152
00:11:58,061 --> 00:12:05,100
It was strong enough to hold a grizzly up to 1,500 pounds and standing over 7 feet tall

153
00:12:05,101 --> 00:12:06,000
on its hind legs.

154
00:12:09,120 --> 00:12:14,280
Although it's been outlawed in the United States for over 100 years, poachers reportedly

155
00:12:14,281 --> 00:12:16,880
still use them for African lions and polar bears.

156
00:12:20,560 --> 00:12:26,760
Even though they're built only for show, Oneida Victor plant manager Rich Butera tests every

157
00:12:26,761 --> 00:12:33,809
number six to make sure it works properly. And it never gets any easier.

158
00:12:33,810 --> 00:12:35,180
It takes three

159
00:12:35,181 --> 00:12:38,567
minutes for Rich to compress the trap springs.

160
00:12:38,568 --> 00:12:41,660
He uses a broom handle to get the leverage

161
00:12:41,661 --> 00:12:43,214
needed to crank them down.

162
00:12:43,215 --> 00:12:46,920
Most nerve-wracking part is making sure you keep your hand out

163
00:12:46,921 --> 00:12:51,100
of the inside that you don't actually slip, because it'll only take one slip.

164
00:12:51,101 --> 00:13:00,320
especially since the jaw set, like many traps of its era, has teeth.

165
00:13:02,540 --> 00:13:05,840
Once the trap set, springing it is literally a snap.

166
00:13:07,800 --> 00:13:11,000
It's just a restraining device to help hold the animal.

167
00:13:11,160 --> 00:13:16,040
It's not looking to really damage and maim anything as much as to restrain it.

168
00:13:16,041 --> 00:13:25,040
Animals caught in foothold traps or snares may struggle and writhe for hours before trappers kill or release them.

169
00:13:27,120 --> 00:13:34,040
To ensure an immediate kill and eliminate such suffering, designers introduced body-gripping traps in the 1950s.

170
00:13:36,840 --> 00:13:40,900
When an animal passes through the trap's jaws and brushes the trigger,

171
00:13:40,901 --> 00:13:46,040
the jaws rotate in opposite directions at a fraction of a second

172
00:13:46,041 --> 00:13:52,160
if properly executed the blow will snap the animal's neck

173
00:13:52,161 --> 00:13:58,120
killing it instantly the dangerous nature of traps like these and many others

174
00:13:58,121 --> 00:14:01,760
has compelled the u.s. government to impose strict regulations

175
00:14:04,760 --> 00:14:08,380
and wildlife officials are working to ensure they're used responsibly

176
00:14:08,381 --> 00:14:16,800
right now the association of fish and wildlife agencies has put together best management

177
00:14:16,801 --> 00:14:22,720
practices based on humane animal welfare aspects of how the animals react to traps

178
00:14:22,721 --> 00:14:29,940
damage to the skin damage to the animal itself and several other factors while snares jaw and

179
00:14:29,941 --> 00:14:33,180
body gripping traps remain the status quo for fur trappers

180
00:14:33,181 --> 00:14:38,360
The rest of the world is hunting for a better trap

181
00:14:38,361 --> 00:14:41,880
One powerful enough to battle an army

182
00:14:41,881 --> 00:14:44,800
Of nocturnal, disease-carrying beasts

183
00:14:44,801 --> 00:14:48,860
It paid to trap beaver in the 1800s

184
00:14:48,861 --> 00:14:51,160
One pelt was worth about $3

185
00:14:51,161 --> 00:14:53,400
Almost $100 today

186
00:14:53,401 --> 00:14:55,980
It could have been traded for many goods

187
00:14:55,981 --> 00:14:57,860
Including one pair of shoes

188
00:14:57,861 --> 00:14:59,840
Two pair of scissors

189
00:14:59,841 --> 00:15:01,240
Two small axes

190
00:15:01,241 --> 00:15:03,040
Or 20 fishhooks

191
00:15:03,041 --> 00:15:05,680
Traps will return on Modern Marvels.

192
00:15:07,220 --> 00:15:10,940
We now return to Traps on Modern Marvels.

193
00:15:12,840 --> 00:15:14,760
Although it's been made of wood,

194
00:15:16,480 --> 00:15:17,180
steel,

195
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:19,020
and clay,

196
00:15:20,240 --> 00:15:22,780
had many different faces,

197
00:15:23,620 --> 00:15:26,240
and dealt many different fates,

198
00:15:27,280 --> 00:15:29,720
its directive is always the same.

199
00:15:30,440 --> 00:15:31,480
Trap mice.

200
00:15:33,040 --> 00:15:37,520
At Canuss Manufacturing in Albia, Iowa,

201
00:15:37,840 --> 00:15:42,540
they're putting a few new twists on this age-old technology.

202
00:15:44,400 --> 00:15:48,320
This model, which catches mice alive, is called the catch-all trap.

203
00:15:50,180 --> 00:15:53,760
When you take a trap and you wind it up,

204
00:15:54,200 --> 00:15:57,060
what you're doing is putting torque on that spring.

205
00:15:57,860 --> 00:16:02,160
The catch-all epitomizes how catching mice doesn't always hinge on using the right bait.

206
00:16:03,040 --> 00:16:08,040
It uses no bait at all, relying instead on expert placement.

207
00:16:08,041 --> 00:16:15,040
To protect itself, a mouse instinctively moves along walls, looking for nooks to hide in.

208
00:16:15,041 --> 00:16:19,040
When it enters the catch-all, it steps onto the trigger.

209
00:16:19,041 --> 00:16:25,040
The trigger activates the spring-loaded three-sided paddle to rotate around the crossbar.

210
00:16:25,041 --> 00:16:30,040
The paddle scoops up the mouse and flips it into the holding compartment.

211
00:16:30,041 --> 00:16:32,200
And then the trap's ready to catch another mouse.

212
00:16:33,740 --> 00:16:38,560
Another mouse, another mouse, another mouse, and another mouse.

213
00:16:38,820 --> 00:16:42,520
Up to 15 to 20 mice, dependable on how it's wound up.

214
00:16:43,520 --> 00:16:46,360
The mice will survive inside the trap for up to 48 hours.

215
00:16:47,040 --> 00:16:49,600
And the trapper can dispose of them however he wants.

216
00:16:52,200 --> 00:16:58,080
After that, they'll either starve or, due to their lack of mobility, die of hypothermia.

217
00:17:00,040 --> 00:17:08,880
Another non-baited caness mousetrap, the Procatch, uses a double-entry teeter-totter trigger to let the mice in and keep them in.

218
00:17:10,960 --> 00:17:16,800
When a mouse enters the Procatch, it scurries onto a platform, hinged at the center.

219
00:17:18,080 --> 00:17:26,440
As the mouse passes the pivot point, its weight causes the platform to tilt on its hinge, which reveals an opening into the trap's holding compartment.

220
00:17:26,441 --> 00:17:32,480
When the mouse steps off the platform, the platform falls back into its set position,

221
00:17:32,980 --> 00:17:34,260
and the exit is blocked.

222
00:17:35,560 --> 00:17:41,200
It is a live, humane trap, but you have to service the trap.

223
00:17:41,380 --> 00:17:45,600
If you want to keep him alive, you put a little water or a little apple,

224
00:17:46,180 --> 00:17:48,120
giving something for nourishment.

225
00:17:49,740 --> 00:17:54,220
And for the purist, Kness has its own variation of the conventional snap trap,

226
00:17:54,221 --> 00:18:00,600
The Snappy. Its dual spring driven striker bar creates extra torque.

227
00:18:01,540 --> 00:18:04,620
That's bad news for the mouse, but a big help for the trapper.

228
00:18:05,560 --> 00:18:10,040
The reason that the people like this trap is because it's easy to set.

229
00:18:10,760 --> 00:18:12,220
Just like that and it's set.

230
00:18:12,760 --> 00:18:18,200
The strike bar is only halfway and it has just halfway to go.

231
00:18:18,201 --> 00:18:27,760
We put this little protector on here to protect the children and pets so that they don't get injured, but it will still kill the mouse.

232
00:18:28,620 --> 00:18:34,160
To cut costs, the company sends the plastic snappy trap pieces out of house for assembly.

233
00:18:36,080 --> 00:18:40,680
Not overseas, but just a half mile down the road, to Ragtime Industries.

234
00:18:40,681 --> 00:18:46,980
Ragtime Industries provides work opportunities to physically and mentally challenged adults

235
00:18:46,981 --> 00:18:50,300
that aren't presently capable of competing for local jobs.

236
00:18:51,560 --> 00:18:56,160
It not only helps keep our costs down in manufacturing and assembly,

237
00:18:56,600 --> 00:18:58,760
but it also gives them a paycheck,

238
00:18:59,500 --> 00:19:04,300
and without them we wouldn't be able to sell as many snap traps as we do.

239
00:19:06,300 --> 00:19:10,100
The Snappy is just another variation of the original snap trap.

240
00:19:10,680 --> 00:19:18,640
The anatomy of a typical snap trap includes a pine board for the base, a metal kill bar

241
00:19:18,641 --> 00:19:28,160
or striker driven by a 15 gauge coil spring, a thin metal trigger rod, and a bait pedal.

242
00:19:28,161 --> 00:19:33,120
When the kill bar is pulled back, tension is applied to the spring.

243
00:19:33,121 --> 00:19:37,840
The trigger rod holds the kill bar in place until a mouse steps on the bait pedal and

244
00:19:37,841 --> 00:19:43,949
releases the latch. The trap size is based on an average length mouse.

245
00:19:43,950 --> 00:19:46,480
The two-inch long striker bar

246
00:19:46,481 --> 00:19:51,280
is designed to clear the bait pedal and land its blow behind the mouse's head as it feeds.

247
00:19:54,780 --> 00:20:00,480
Ralph Waldo Emerson once mused, build a better mousetrap and the world will beat a path to your

248
00:20:00,481 --> 00:20:04,270
door. He was speaking about capitalism.

249
00:20:04,271 --> 00:20:08,740
But countless individuals have taken his words

250
00:20:08,741 --> 00:20:15,100
literally, in hopes that the world would bring them a big piece of the $1.3 billion a year

251
00:20:15,101 --> 00:20:17,124
pest control pie.

252
00:20:17,125 --> 00:20:25,100
In 1894, inventor William Hooker was the first to cash in, creating

253
00:20:25,101 --> 00:20:27,180
what became the first patented snap trap.

254
00:20:28,600 --> 00:20:31,320
Its iconic design remains the industry standard.

255
00:20:33,160 --> 00:20:36,100
What might be history's first humane mousetrap

256
00:20:36,101 --> 00:20:40,340
was used as early as the 3rd century B.C. in parts of Asia.

257
00:20:41,360 --> 00:20:46,160
This is an early pottery mousetrap.

258
00:20:46,161 --> 00:20:49,660
And when the mouse goes in through the door,

259
00:20:49,900 --> 00:20:53,340
goes back and eats through the thread

260
00:20:53,341 --> 00:20:55,580
while he's devouring the bait,

261
00:20:56,240 --> 00:20:58,440
then the door will then drop down.

262
00:20:58,980 --> 00:21:02,220
There are air holes punched through the pottery

263
00:21:02,221 --> 00:21:04,960
to keep the mouse alive.

264
00:21:07,120 --> 00:21:09,840
Another popular variety is less subtle.

265
00:21:11,680 --> 00:21:14,880
Crushing traps are based on an even more ancient trap design,

266
00:21:15,720 --> 00:21:16,820
the deadfall.

267
00:21:18,220 --> 00:21:21,300
Historically, deadfalls have been used to kill animals of all sizes.

268
00:21:21,301 --> 00:21:24,940
This is a deadfall trap.

269
00:21:25,460 --> 00:21:30,740
It has three blocks of wood which are held up by a burlap string,

270
00:21:31,160 --> 00:21:36,580
and when the mouse or the rat chew through the burlap string,

271
00:21:36,920 --> 00:21:39,680
the block of wood will come down and crush him.

272
00:21:41,960 --> 00:21:44,800
Back at the North American Trap Collectors Museum,

273
00:21:45,540 --> 00:21:50,220
President Tom Parr demonstrates how a more complicated mousetrap isn't always better.

